
  Runner: Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m with [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m, via [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. Zero dependencies,
  which keeps the repo's dependency-free character intact, and it matches the CommonJS already in [38;5;153msrc/[39m.

  That requires one accommodation in [38;5;153mprefs.js[39m: a footer that exports for CommonJS when [38;5;153mmodule[39m exists, so the same file
  works as a browser global and as a [38;5;153mrequire()[39m-able module. And the storage backend needs a seam — the availability
  probe runs once at load, so the module reads [38;5;153mglobalThis.localStorage[39m through an internal [38;5;153minit(store)[39m that tests can
  call again with a fake. That seam is the only thing in the design that exists for testability rather than for the
  feature; I think it earns its place, since the interesting failures are exactly the ones a real browser won't
  reproduce on demand.

  Tests in [38;5;153mtest/prefs.test.js[39m, against a fake store (a [38;5;153mMap[39m wrapper) that can be told to throw:

  - round-trip: [38;5;153mset[39m then [38;5;153mget[39m returns the value; [38;5;153mget[39m on a missing key returns the fallback
  - [38;5;153mremove[39m deletes one preference and leaves the others
  - [38;5;153mclear[39m empties the namespace
  - corrupt JSON in the entry → [38;5;153mget[39m returns the fallback instead of throwing, and the entry is reset
  - a non-object stored under the key (e.g. [38;5;153m"null"[39m, [38;5;153m"[]"[39m) → same treatment
  - backend throws on write (quota) → [38;5;153mset[39m returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m, no throw
  - backend throws on read (security) → module falls back to in-memory, [38;5;153mget[39m/[38;5;153mset[39m still work
  - values survive as types: a boolean stays a boolean, not [38;5;153m"true"[39m

